- document that fan-out changes cannot be tested on a branch. `on: workflow_run`
  always executes the DEFAULT BRANCH's copy of trigger-downstream.yml, so a
  branch that adds or removes a [[trigger-downstream]], reorders `needs`, edits
  `triggers`, or repins a consumer `ref` gets none of it: the old graph runs and
  the change first takes effect at merge. The `validate` job is only a partial
  net -- it checks out the tested head SHA and runs --check, so it catches "you
  edited the manifest and forgot to regenerate", but it cannot exercise a fan-out
  the running orchestrator does not contain. The failure mode is a green
  downstream/<lane> status computed from the previous job list, which reads as
  proof and is not.

  Accepted for now: the cost scales with how often the dependency GRAPH changes,
  not with how many repos are in it, and the graph is expected to change rarely
  even as the repo count grows. Revisit if that stops being true.

  The fix, when it is worth it, is a dynamic orchestrator: replace the generated
  per-consumer `uses:` jobs with one job that reads the manifest at
  workflow_run.head_sha and fans out through dispatch-and-wait, which unlike
  `uses:` accepts a runtime ref. Both paths already exist -- dispatch-and-wait
  drives every public->private edge today -- so this is a change of default, not
  new machinery. It costs the native reusable-workflow nesting on public->public
  edges: consumer jobs would render in the consumer's Actions tab rather than
  inline in the caller's run, and gating would go back to polling run status.

