- HPC job paths are keyed on the artifact name alone, so two runs building the
  same artifact collide. RemotePaths.derive(work_dir, artifact_name) gives both
  hpc-jobs/<artifact>.out (jobscript's #SBATCH --output) and staging/<artifact>/,
  under which plan_remote_prefixes unpacks the deps as deps/<i>. submit-wait then
  TAILS that .out live, so a concurrent run's job writes into the file this run is
  reading and this run reports the other job's outcome.

  Seen on ecmwf/ecflow run 32743760455, build-hpc (atos-hpc-nvidia): its own job
  37369545 was memory-killed at 15:27:40, then at 15:28:15 job 37370645
  (--comment=32744666086-1, i.e. a DIFFERENT github run) appeared in the same .out
  and its "Could not find a package configuration file provided by ecbuild"
  became this run's reported failure. The tell is right in the error text -- the
  run blames a job id it never submitted. Hours went into the find_package red
  herring before the job ids gave it away. The shared staging/<artifact>/deps is
  very likely why that second job could not find ecbuild in the first place: the
  two runs race on one deps dir.

  Not a simple "add the run id" fix: the artifact-keyed name is load-bearing for
  reattach (find_active_job_by_name is how a re-run adopts an in-flight job).
  Needs a deliberate split -- reattach keyed on the SLURM job name, but per-run
  output and deps paths -- plus a decision on what a reattaching run should tail.
  Minimum viable improvement meanwhile: have submit-wait verify the job id in the
  output it reports against the one it submitted, and say so loudly when they
  differ, so this never again presents as a build error.

- -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH in the HPC recipes is inert, and is a trap the day the
  environment stops covering for it. jobscript.py:172 exports CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH
  colon-joined (plan_remote_prefixes returns ":".join(...)), which is correct for
  an ENVIRONMENT variable. But ecflow's four recipes
  (.ci/hpc/build-{gnu,intel,aocc,nvidia}.sh) then pass
  -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH="$CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH", and a -D CACHE variable is a
  SEMICOLON-separated list -- so the colon-joined string arrives as one path that
  does not exist.

  Verified against a real ecbuild install: -D colon + env set -> found (the env
  var rescues it); -D semicolon + env empty -> found; -D colon + env EMPTY ->
  "Could not find a package configuration file provided by ecbuild". So today the
  flag does nothing, and any build whose env var is cleared or empty (a no-deps
  build exports an empty value) fails with a message that points at the package
  rather than at the separator.

  Fix either way: drop the -D from the recipes and let the env var do the work, or
  emit the value semicolon-joined for -D use. Do not leave both halves disagreeing.

